TYPES OF SCREWS
Head shape controls appearance, flush vs proud, and bearing surface.
Drive type controls torque, cam-out resistance, and tamper resistance.
Point and thread design determine whether the fastener needs a pilot hole, cuts its own threads, or drills as it goes.
Material and coating matter: wood vs metal vs masonry vs outdoor exposure.
A bolt typically uses a nut or tapped hole; a screw usually threads directly into the material.

